feat(windows): split maximize toggle into maximize + unmaximize cmds

Toggle semantics make the cmd panel surprising — a user typing
"maximize" expects maximize, not "if-already-maximized-do-the-opposite".
Two separate commands, both non-toggling.

- `tile:window:maximize` no longer toggles. It snapshots the window's
  current bounds into the windowRegistry params (`preMaximizeBounds`),
  then resizes via `setBounds(workArea)` and calls
  `BrowserWindow.maximize()` for OSes that honour it. No-op if already
  maximized (registry-stashed or Electron-flagged).
- `tile:window:unmaximize` is new. Restores the stashed bounds via
  `setBounds`, falling back to `BrowserWindow.unmaximize()` if the
  registry has nothing recorded (window was OS-maximized via dbl-click).
- `api.window.unmaximize(id?)` exposed on the tile preload alongside
  `api.window.maximize`. Same `window.manage` capability gate.
- `windows` feature: new `unmaximize window` cmd-panel command pairs
  with the existing `maximize window`.

Why bounds-stash rather than relying on Electron alone: frameless /
panel-style windows on macOS don't reliably flip the internal
isMaximized flag when `BrowserWindow.maximize()` is called, so a
subsequent `unmaximize()` is a no-op on them — meaning we can't trust
the OS flag alone for tile windows. The same workaround is already used
in `ipc.ts` for the open-time `maximize: true` path.

Tests pin both halves of the contract — maximize twice keeps the window
maximized; unmaximize restores the pre-maximize bounds (x/y/width/height)
exactly.
